[2026-09-24T00:50:35.135+08:00] [InterfaceDesigner][ROOT] tool-result> read_file result="content=\" 1  ---\\n 2  name: non-leaf-ui-only-design\\n 3  description: Use for ARC InterfaceDesigner work when a parent node only owns visual shell, layout reference behavior, route slots, or child mount surfaces. This is intentionally weak design, not feature implementation.\\n 4  ---\\n 5  \\n 6  # non-leaf-ui-only-design\\n 7  \\n 8  Use this skill when you determine the current node owns only visual shell, layout, route slots, navigation surface, or mount-point behavior.\\n 9  \\n10  Non-leaf UI-only design must stay thin. The purpose is to give children a stable visual/composition boundary, not to pre-implement child features or create test-heavy behavior.\\n11  \\n12  ## Instructions\\n13  \\n14  1. Use this skill only when the non-leaf node has visual references or explicit parent-owned UI composition requirements.\\n15  2. Focus on visual shell, layout hierarchy, navigation surface, container structure, and presentation scaffolding.\\n16  3. Match visual reference structure where present: ordering, grouping, spacing, density, major visible regions, and style language.\\n17  4. Do not implement child-owned business behavior or detailed runtime interactions.\\n18  5. If visual references exist, materialize the UI shell files and style boundary that preserve the parent composition; otherwise return no interfaces and do not read or edit files.\\n19  6. Prefer one or two broad interface contracts for the shell or mount boundary; avoid one interface per visual subsection.\\n20  7. Interface specifications should describe stable regions and allowed child attachment points, not exact child copy, form internals, route-transition mechanics, or backend data loops.\\n21  8. Do not create API, FUNC, or DB contracts for non-leaf UI-only design.\\n22  9. Keep `test_focus` minimal: smoke-level renderability, presence of the parent shell, and absence of fake child data are enough.\\n23  10. The interface `type` must be one of `UI`, `API`, `FUNC`, or `DB`
